# =============================================================================
# Adaptive Revenue Intelligence Engine — environment template
# Copy to .env and fill in. NEVER commit .env.
# =============================================================================
# NOTE: M0 (the benchmark that proves the thesis) requires NONE of these.
# `make bench` runs fully offline with zero credentials. These are for M1.
# =============================================================================

# --- Database (Supabase) -----------------------------------------------------
# Use the POOLED connection string for workers (many short-lived connections).
# Supabase dashboard → Project Settings → Database → Connection string → "Transaction pooler"
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.PROJECT_REF:PASSWORD@aws-0-REGION.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres

# Direct (session) connection — used ONLY for running migrations.
DATABASE_DIRECT_URL=postgresql://postgres.PROJECT_REF:PASSWORD@aws-0-REGION.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres

# --- integration-test database (never a deployment) --------------------------
#
# `make test-all` reads TEST_DATABASE_URL and NEVER falls back to DATABASE_URL.
# Unset means the integration suite skips; there is no combination of variables
# that routes it back to a deployment. This is structural, not a convention:
# the fixtures had been reading DATABASE_URL, which meant they wrote to the
# deployed Supabase database, and the deployed worker claimed their jobs within
# a second of ingestion and processed them with its own handlers.
#
# Point this at a database NO deployment is attached to. Locally that means a
# different database on the Compose Postgres than the stack itself uses
# (`arie_test` vs. `arie`), so the Compose workers cannot see test jobs even
# while running.
#
#   docker compose up -d db
#   export TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://arie:arie_local_dev@localhost:5432/arie_test
#   export ARIE_ALLOW_INTEGRATION_TEST_DB=1
#   make test-db      # creates the database if needed, stamps the marker
#   make test-all
#
# Two further guards: ARIE_ALLOW_INTEGRATION_TEST_DB=1 records intent, and the
# target must carry the marker table only `make test-db` creates — a command
# that refuses to stamp anything matching DATABASE_URL or already holding data.
TEST_DATABASE_URL=
TEST_DATABASE_DIRECT_URL=
ARIE_ALLOW_INTEGRATION_TEST_DB=

SUPABASE_URL=https://PROJECT_REF.supabase.co
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# --- LLM providers -----------------------------------------------------------
# M1 Step 10. Used for ONE narrow task: buying-signal extraction from free
# text (arie.llm) — never a general "call an LLM" facility. Leave
# DEEPSEEK_API_KEY blank and everything still works: arie.llm.baseline (the
# deterministic comparison point) needs no key, arie.jobs.worker has no
# handler that calls arie.llm at all yet, and bench/llm_signal_eval.py
# reports the baseline only and exits 0 — nothing in CI or make test needs
# this key set.
DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=
DEEPSEEK_BASE_URL=https://api.deepseek.com
# deepseek-chat (cheap tier) only — never escalated to deepseek-reasoner.
# Changing this is a real methodology change, not a knob to tune per lead.
DEEPSEEK_MODEL=deepseek-chat
DEEPSEEK_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30
# Total attempts including the first. A malformed response is retried with
# the identical request (stateless — no multi-turn self-correction), not a
# reasoning loop.
DEEPSEEK_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3

# --- M7 intelligence layer ---------------------------------------------------
# The GENERAL "call a model" facility the block above is deliberately not
# (arie.llm.provider / factory / structured / budget / service). Backend only —
# none of these is NEXT_PUBLIC_ or reachable from the browser.
#
# Nothing here needs to be set to run the test suite: LLM_PROVIDER=fake is a
# real selectable provider (arie.llm.fake_provider) that needs no credential,
# and every M7 feature degrades to deterministic behaviour when the layer is
# unavailable rather than failing.
#
#   deepseek  the real hosted model
#   fake      the deterministic in-process double (tests, keyless dev)
#   none      switched off; every AI-assisted feature falls back
# An unrecognised value raises at startup rather than silently selecting a
# default — a typo that quietly became "deepseek" would spend real money.
LLM_PROVIDER=deepseek
# Must be a key of arie.ledger.pricing.MODEL_PRICES, or its calls could not be
# ledgered. Checked at provider construction, not on first customer request.
LLM_MODEL=deepseek-chat
# Provider-neutral credential/endpoint. Both fall back to the DEEPSEEK_-named
# variables above (there is one DeepSeek account and one key); these exist so
# that swapping LLM_PROVIDER later does not require the credential to keep
# living under a DeepSeek name.
LLM_API_KEY=
LLM_BASE_URL=
LLM_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30
# Total attempts per structured generation, including the first — so 2 is
# "one bounded repair retry" and no more.
LLM_MAX_ATTEMPTS=2
# Ceiling on one call's output tokens. Every M7 output is a small bounded JSON
# document; a model generating unbounded prose is malfunctioning.
LLM_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=2000
# Total characters of customer/third-party text that may be fenced into one
# prompt. Enforced at the prompt-rendering boundary (arie.llm.structured), so
# "do not send an entire huge CSV to the model" is structural, not a rule each
# call site has to remember.
LLM_MAX_UNTRUSTED_CHARS=20000
#
# Per-organization AI ceilings are NOT environment variables. They are columns
# on `organizations` (migrations/0034), defaulted for every organization and
# read by arie.llm.budget: max_llm_calls_per_batch, max_llm_cost_usd_per_batch,
# max_llm_cost_usd_per_month, preferred_llm_model.

# Optional stronger tier for the escalation path of the model cascade. Neither
# is read by any code in this repo today — the cascade escalates no further
# than DeepSeek — so both are placeholders for a tier that isn't built.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# --- Enrichment providers ----------------------------------------------------
# PROVIDER_MODE=simulated (default) replays the frozen eval corpus — no key
# needed, no real spend possible, and only corpus identities can be enriched.
# PROVIDER_MODE=live (post-M1 P5) enriches ANY ingested lead through the one
# real adapter below. Explicit opt-in only: build_handlers() raises
# AbstractCompanyConfigurationError at worker startup if this is set without
# the key configured — never a silent fallback to the simulator.
#
# Live V1 Foundation: a lead enriched by a real provider can NEVER be routed or
# rejected autonomously. It always terminates at AWAITING_HUMAN (or
# SHADOW_EVALUATED if ingested as shadow), because the confidence threshold
# gating autonomy is calibrated on synthetic data and has not been validated
# against real provider evidence. This is deliberately NOT configurable — see
# arie.live.safety. Simulated mode is unaffected and still auto-routes.
PROVIDER_MODE=simulated

# Abstract API — Company Enrichment (arie.providers.live_abstract). Free tier:
# 100 requests/month, no card required. https://docs.abstractapi.com/api/company-enrichment
ABSTRACT_COMPANY_API_KEY=
ABSTRACT_COMPANY_BASE_URL=https://companyenrichment.abstractapi.com/v2/
ABSTRACT_COMPANY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10
# An ESTIMATED unit cost (Standard plan list price / requests-per-month at
# time of writing), not a per-call price the provider reports — see
# LiveProviderConfig.cost_usd_per_call's own docstring.
ABSTRACT_COMPANY_COST_USD_PER_CALL=0.00165

# --- live spend caps (Live V1 Foundation) ------------------------------------
#
# Hard ceilings enforced by arie.live.budget BEFORE any real provider call,
# against the durable provider_calls ledger — so they hold across workers,
# restarts, and concurrent jobs rather than per-process. A call that would
# exceed either cap is not made: the lead stops with a
# per_lead_budget_exhausted / daily_budget_exhausted stop reason and goes to a
# human, rather than being decided on partial evidence.
#
# SERVER-SIDE ONLY. Never expose these to a browser (no NEXT_PUBLIC_/VITE_
# variant) — the frontend is a separate repository and has no business knowing
# the exact shape of the spend ceiling.
#
# Defaults are sized to be safe for someone who deploys without reading this:
# $2.00/day is ~1,200 Abstract lookups (far above any demo volume, far below an
# amount worth losing to a stuck queue overnight), and $0.05/lead is ~30. A
# single lead needing more than that is a bug, not a deep enrichment.
#
# Constraint: per-lead must not exceed daily, or one lead could drain the day
# and the daily cap would be decorative. LiveBudgetConfig refuses that at
# startup rather than discovering it in production.
LIVE_PROVIDER_DAILY_BUDGET_USD=2.00
LIVE_PROVIDER_PER_LEAD_BUDGET_USD=0.05

# --- live provider strategy (multi-provider V1) ------------------------------
#
# How PROVIDER_MODE=live walks its three providers. SERVER-SIDE ONLY, and
# structurally ignored by PROVIDER_MODE=simulated (the public demo) — only the
# live handler builder ever reads these.
#
#   optimized (DEFAULT)  — providers called selectively and sequentially,
#     cheapest first (Abstract -> Hunter -> Apollo), stopping as soon as the
#     existing evidence/confidence logic says more evidence is unnecessary.
#     This is ARIE operating; calling every provider for every lead would
#     defeat its purpose.
#
#   evaluation_parallel  — PRIVATE experiment mode: after company enrichment,
#     both person providers are called CONCURRENTLY for the same lead so their
#     coverage, quality, latency, credits, and agreement can be measured on
#     identical inputs. Deliberately spends more per lead (see the explicit
#     evaluation budget below), never autonomous, identifies itself on the
#     receipt (versions.policy = live_evaluation_parallel). Not an operating
#     mode; never make it the default; never enable it for the public demo.
LIVE_PROVIDER_STRATEGY=optimized

# Optional experiment knob: comma-separated provider names overriding the
# default acquisition order for the OPTIMIZED strategy (priority only — it
# cannot exclude a provider; unknown names refuse to start). Leave empty for
# the cheapest-first default. Example testing order B:
#   LIVE_PROVIDER_ORDER=abstract_company_enrichment,apollo_person_enrichment,hunter_combined_enrichment
LIVE_PROVIDER_ORDER=

# Per-lead cap for evaluation_parallel runs, which intentionally buy
# overlapping evidence. A separate explicit number, NOT a bypass: the same
# LiveSpendGuard enforces it, the daily cap above still applies, and
# LiveBudgetConfig refuses a value above the daily cap at startup.
LIVE_EVALUATION_PER_LEAD_BUDGET_USD=0.10

# How long a provider that returned a credit/quota-exhausted error is left
# uncalled (read durably from provider_calls.error_kind, so it holds across
# workers and restarts — see arie.live.cooldown). 3600 = probe a dead quota
# ~hourly instead of once per lead; 0 disables the cooldown.
LIVE_PROVIDER_QUOTA_COOLDOWN_SECONDS=3600

# Apollo — People Enrichment (arie.providers.live_apollo). REQUIRED for
# PROVIDER_MODE=live: the live worker builds both real adapters at startup and
# refuses to start with either key missing, rather than silently running a
# half-blind pipeline that still reports coverage and cost.
# https://docs.apollo.io/reference/people-enrichment
#
# POST /api/v1/people/match, authenticated by an x-api-key HEADER (never a
# query parameter), matched on the lead's work email. Supplies title_seniority
# and title_function — 35 of the scorer's 100 reachable points, permanently
# unknown for every live lead before this provider existed.
#
# Called SECOND and only when company enrichment left the decision open, so a
# lead that is already a confident reject on firmographics never pays for it.
APOLLO_API_KEY=
APOLLO_BASE_URL=https://api.apollo.io/api/v1/people/match
APOLLO_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10
# A MODELLED USD equivalent of the ONE CREDIT Apollo consumes for a match — not
# a dollar figure Apollo reports, and not billed spend. Derived from the
# published Basic plan ($49/month for 2,500 credits/month => $0.0196/credit);
# see ApolloPersonConfig.cost_usd_per_success for what it does and does not
# claim. A no-match response consumes no credit and is ledgered at zero.
APOLLO_PERSON_COST_USD_PER_SUCCESS=0.0196

# Hunter — Combined Enrichment (arie.providers.live_hunter). REQUIRED for
# PROVIDER_MODE=live: the live worker builds every registered adapter at
# startup and refuses to start with any key missing.
# https://hunter.io/api-documentation/v2
#
# GET /v2/combined/find?email=..., authenticated by an X-API-KEY HEADER (never
# a query parameter), returning a Clearbit-style person+company pair. Supplies
# title_seniority/title_function as the CHEAPER of the two person providers
# (0.2 credits per successful enrichment), so it is tried before Apollo; the
# company half rides along for the provider bake-off only and is not persisted
# as evidence. A no-match is a 404 and consumes no credits.
# Free plan: 50 credits/month (~250 enrichments at 0.2 each).
HUNTER_API_KEY=
HUNTER_BASE_URL=https://api.hunter.io/v2/combined/find
HUNTER_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10
# A MODELLED USD equivalent of the 0.2 credits Hunter consumes per successful
# enrichment — not a billed figure. Starter plan list price at time of writing:
# $49/month for 2,000 credits/month => $0.0245/credit => $0.0049 per success.
# See HunterConfig.cost_usd_per_success for the full derivation.
HUNTER_ENRICHMENT_COST_USD=0.0049

# Deferred — no adapter written; this is an unused placeholder.
FIRECRAWL_API_KEY=

# --- Shadow mode (post-M1 P5) ------------------------------------------------
# Not a server-wide env var on purpose — shadow-ness is per-lead, set at
# ingestion via POST /leads {"mode": "shadow"} (default "normal"), so ARIE can
# sit beside an existing production workflow lead-by-lead rather than an
# all-or-nothing switch. See README's "Shadow mode" section.

# --- Policy configuration ----------------------------------------------------
# Hard per-lead spend ceiling (defense in depth, independent of the EVoI math).
LEAD_BUDGET_USD_CAP=1.50
# Target error rate for autonomous decisions; drives conformal threshold selection.
TARGET_AUTONOMOUS_ERROR_RATE=0.10
# Latency penalty weight in the EVoI formula (USD per second).
LATENCY_PENALTY_USD_PER_SEC=0.01

# --- Observability -----------------------------------------------------------
# Setting an OTLP endpoint IS what enables tracing — there is deliberately no
# separate on/off flag (two switches for one behaviour is two things to get out
# of sync). Left blank, the API and worker create no spans at all rather than
# building them and throwing them away. Point it at a collector's HTTP receiver,
# e.g. http://localhost:4318
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=
OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=arie
LANGFUSE_HOST=
LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY=
LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY=

# --- Local demo (Productization M2B) -----------------------------------------
# An ARIE organization API key scoped to leads:write, leads:read, reviews:read,
# reviews:write — minted via `python -m scripts.mint_api_key` (see that
# script's docstring), never through this file. `scripts/demo/cli.py` reads
# this and sends it as `Authorization: Bearer <key>`; unset, the demo now
# fails fast with a clear error instead of hitting the API unauthenticated.
ARIE_DEMO_API_KEY=

# =============================================================================
# Productization M6 — the commercial layer
# =============================================================================
# Everything below is OPTIONAL to run the system. Left unset:
#   - no Stripe call is ever attempted, and the billing endpoints refuse with a
#     clear 4xx instead of half-working;
#   - transactional email goes to arie.email.fake.FakeEmailSender, which never
#     opens a socket (local dev and CI default — no email can escape);
#   - Turnstile verification is bypassed, which is a documented dev/CI seam and
#     NOT a bypass in an environment where the secret IS configured.
# The Legacy Organization is grandfathered onto the `internal` plan by
# migrations/0030 and needs none of this to keep working.

# --- Stripe ------------------------------------------------------------------
# SERVER-SIDE ONLY. Every Stripe call is made from this process; the browser
# never holds a Stripe key and never names a price — it names an ARIE plan
# ("starter"/"growth"/"pro") and the server translates it through the price ids
# below (see StripeConfig.price_id_for_plan). A client-supplied price id is
# never trusted, because it would let a caller subscribe itself to any price in
# the account, including one that costs nothing.
#
# Start in TEST MODE (sk_test_...). Test and live mode have entirely separate
# objects: a live price id will not resolve against a test key, and a webhook
# signed by one mode's secret will not verify against the other's.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# From the Stripe dashboard's webhook endpoint (Developers -> Webhooks), NOT
# the API key. Verification is local HMAC over the raw request body, so this is
# what makes POST /billing/webhook trustworthy at all — unset, that endpoint
# rejects everything rather than trusting unsigned input.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Recurring Price ids (price_..., not product ids), one per purchasable plan.
# `internal` is deliberately absent: it is grandfathered, never sold.
STRIPE_PRICE_STARTER=
STRIPE_PRICE_GROWTH=
STRIPE_PRICE_PRO=

# --- Transactional email (AhaSend) -------------------------------------------
# Both values must be set for real sending; either one missing keeps the fake
# sender, so a half-configured environment cannot email real people by
# accident. Used for invitations, human-review-required notifications, and
# usage warnings — never marketing, never bulk.
AHASEND_API_KEY=
AHASEND_ACCOUNT_ID=
AHASEND_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10
# Must be a sender the AhaSend account has verified for the domain, or every
# send is rejected at the provider.
EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS=notifications@arie.invalid
EMAIL_FROM_NAME=ARIE

# --- Cloudflare Turnstile ----------------------------------------------------
# Abuse protection in front of self-service organization provisioning
# (POST /organizations). TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY is public and belongs in the
# frontend's own build config too; TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY never leaves this
# process.
TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=
TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=

# --- Console links in email --------------------------------------------------
# Absolute base URL of the customer-facing console, used to build invitation
# accept links and review-required links when a caller supplies no return URL
# of its own. Not a secret. Unset, those emails carry no working link — set it
# before enabling real email.
FRONTEND_BASE_URL=

# --- Notification thresholds -------------------------------------------------
# Fraction of a monthly limit (leads or modeled spend) that triggers ONE usage
# warning per organization per calendar month; dedup rides the audit log, not a
# new table.
USAGE_WARNING_THRESHOLD_PCT=0.8

# --- Worker liveness ---------------------------------------------------------
# A DB-backed "is anything actually consuming the queue" signal, read by
# GET /healthz/worker. Deliberately separate from /healthz: a dead worker fleet
# is a real problem, but it must not flap the API's own liveness monitor, which
# can do nothing to fix it. stale_after is generous relative to the interval
# (4x) so one slow poll cycle under load doesn't flap the monitor either.
WORKER_HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL_SECONDS=15
WORKER_HEARTBEAT_STALE_AFTER_SECONDS=60

# --- Runtime -----------------------------------------------------------------
LOG_LEVEL=INFO
WORKER_POLL_INTERVAL_SEC=2
WORKER_MAX_ATTEMPTS=4
# How long a claimed job stays locked before another worker may reclaim it.
WORKER_LEASE_SECONDS=300
